DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT STRATEGY 
1. Zero Downtime During Peak Hours (Revenue Protection)
Advantage: LocalWP allows developing and offline testing, which eliminates the risk of breaking the live site during business-critical hours.
Business Impact: 
Avoids loss of sales-The bakery’s current site likely drives foot traffic via daily specials or seasonal menus. Even minor live issues could cause errors that turn away customers.
Preserves reputation- A site crash during peak hours could damage customers trust in their online ordering system.
Technical Alignment: Test new features, such as online ordering, in isolation without affecting the live payment processing system.

2. Safe Payment Integration Testing (Risk Mitigation)
Advantage: LocalWP’s staging-like environment lets developers simulate transactions without processing real payments or exposing customer data.
Business Impact:
Prevents failed transactions-Testing gateways (e.g., Stripe, Square) locally avoids declined orders or duplicate charges on the live site.
Compliance security-Avoids PCI-DSS violations during development by keeping test transactions offline.
Technical Alignment: Use LocalWP’s Live Links to share test payment flows with the bakery owner for approval before going live.

3. Efficient Workflow for Non-Technical Teams
Advantage:LocalWP’s one-click WordPress setup and pre-configured PHP/MySQL reduce dependency on external developers for minor updates.
Business Impact:
Faster launches-The bakery staff can preview blog posts or testimonials locally before publishing, reducing back-and-forth with freelancers.
Cost control-Avoids paying for staging hosting or developer hours spent debugging live-site conflicts.
Technical Alignment: LocalWP’s Blueprints feature lets the bakery save their ideal theme/plugin setup for future updates (e.g., seasonal menu templates).

WORDPRESS ARCHITECTURE DECISION
Recommended Approach:Custom Post Types (CPTs) for Products and Testimonials & default posts for blog content
1.Content Management Workflow
Products (Pastries/Cakes)- Requires unique fields (price, ingredients, dietary tags). CPT with Advanced Custom Fields. Separate admin menu (Products vs. Posts) prevents staff confusion when adding new items.
Testimonials-Needs structured data such as customer name, rating, photo, and CPT ensures testimonials don’t mix with blog posts.
Blog Posts-Default posts are enough (categories/tags handle recipe updates, bakery news).
Business Impact-Employees can manage products/testimonials without accidentally altering blog content.

2. SEO Implications
Dedicated URL Structures- /products/croissant/ vs. /blog/croissant-recipe/ avoids keyword cannibalization.Testimonials can be excluded from search if needed.
Schema Markup- CPTs enable product-specific schema (price, availability) for snippets.
Default Posts-Categories (/blog/recipes/, /blog/events/) maintain SEO-friendly hierarchies.
Business Impact-Products rank separately from blog content.

3. Future Scalability
CPTs are flexible because:
Adding Content Types-Future needs (e.g., Workshops or Catering) can be new CPTs without hacking Posts.
Performance- Separating products/posts reduces database bloat.
Theme Development-CPT-specific templates keep code modular.

THEME DEVELOPMENT PLANNING
For Products: single-product.php → single.php → singular.php → index.php
For Blog Posts: single-post.php → single.php → singular.php → index.php
For Testimonials: archive-testimonial.php → archive.php → index.php
